---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import { ContextWindowDemo } from "../../components/ContextWindowDemo";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Context Windows - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="context-windows">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				A context window is the total amount of text a model can "see" at once. Think of it as the
				model's working memory: everything it considers when generating a response has to fit inside
				this window. Your system prompt, the conversation history, any files or documents you've
				included, and the response it's generating all compete for space in the same fixed-size
				window.
			</p>

			<p>
				Context windows are measured in <strong class="text-stone-800">tokens</strong>, not words.
				Tokens are chunks of text, roughly 3/4 of a word on average. A 200,000 token context window
				can hold roughly 150,000 words, or around 500 pages of text. Some frontier models now go further, offering windows of around 1,000,000 tokens. That sounds like a lot, and it is, but it fills up faster than you'd think when you're including code files, documentation,
				examples, tool calls, and conversation history. Bigger isn't automatically better either: a focused 200,000 tokens of relevant context usually beats a million tokens of noise, so that's still what we aim for.
			</p>

			<h3>
				What Happens When You Hit the Limit
			</h3>

			<p>
				Different tools handle context limits differently, but they all have to deal with the same
				fundamental problem: conversations grow, but the window doesn't.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Compaction</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Most tools will automatically summarize or compress older parts of the conversation to
						make room. Claude Code, for example, will compact earlier messages into shorter
						summaries when the context gets large. This keeps the conversation going but means
						details from earlier exchanges may be lost or simplified.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Truncation</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Some tools simply drop the oldest messages. If your conversation is 50 messages long and
						the window can only hold 30, the first 20 may be silently removed. You'll still see
						them in your chat history, but the model won't.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Hard stops</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Some tools will simply tell you the conversation is too long and ask you to start a new
						one. This is the least graceful approach but at least it's honest about the limitation.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<ContextWindowDemo client:visible />

			<h3>
				Recency Bias
			</h3>

			<p>
				Even within the context window, not all content is treated equally. Models tend to pay more
				attention to what's at the <strong class="text-stone-800">beginning</strong> and <strong
					class="text-stone-800">end</strong
				> of the context, with content in the middle getting less attention. This is sometimes called the
				"lost in the middle" problem.
			</p>

			<p>
				In practice, this means that in a long conversation, the model will most reliably remember
				your system prompt (at the top) and your most recent messages (at the bottom). Instructions
				or context buried in the middle of a 40-message conversation? Those may effectively become
				invisible.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical impact">
				<p>
					If you gave the AI important instructions 30 messages ago and it's now ignoring them, it's
					likely not being stubborn — it genuinely can't "see" those instructions anymore. Either
					they've been compacted away or they're lost in the middle. Restate important context in
					your current message, or start a fresh conversation.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Callout variant="success" title="Mitigation technique">
				<p>
					Instead of pasting important instructions directly into the conversation (where they'll
					eventually be compacted away), reference them as files the model's tools can
					look up on demand. Keeping your standards and project context in an{" "}
					<code>AGENTS.md</code> file, a skill, or project documentation
					means the
					tool can re-read those instructions whenever it needs them, even after earlier context has
					been summarized away. A file reference is also more likely to be flagged as important during
					compaction and kept in the window longer than inline instructions.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-2">
					This isn't foolproof; compaction can still lose track of file references, and the model may
					not always choose to re-read them. Treat this as a useful supplement, not a replacement for
					starting fresh conversations when things drift.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>
				Working Within the Window
			</h3>

			<p>
				Understanding context windows changes how you use AI tools. Here are the patterns that work
				best:
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<Callout variant="tip" title="Keep conversations focused">
					<p>
						One task per conversation. Don't ask about your database schema, then pivot to CSS
						debugging, then ask about deployment configs. Each topic shift adds noise that dilutes
						the model's attention on your current problem.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Front-load important context">
					<p>
						Put your most important information in your first message. This
						takes advantage of the model's tendency to pay more attention to the beginning of the
						context. Tools like Cursor and Claude Code do this automatically with project files like
						AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Start fresh when it drifts">
					<p>
						If the AI starts "forgetting" things, repeating itself, or ignoring your instructions,
						the context window is likely the culprit. Start a new conversation. Copy in the
						relevant context. You'll usually get better results than trying to course-correct a
						bloated conversation.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Reference documentation and files for important but longer details.">
					<p>
						Including references allows the AI to pull down, summarize the items it needs (especially when they have sub-agents) and
						keep the conversation focused on the current task. It also allows the AI to re-read items to push it into the conversation when most needed.
					</p>
				</Callout>
			</div>

			<p>
				Context windows are one of the biggest practical constraints when working with AI tools.
				They're getting larger with each model generation, but they'll always be finite. Learning to
				work within that constraint and being intentional about what context you provide and when to
				start fresh is one of the most impactful skills you can develop.
			</p>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
